<%# locals: (back_label:, back_path:, page:) %>
<%# The crumb line of a page below a section of the management area:
    "‹ Rollen und Rechte / Neue Rolle". back_label and back_path lead to the
    section or tab the page belongs to, page names the page itself.

    Where it stands: right under the page head (organisations/shared/_settings_subnav),
    under the tab row where the section has one, before the content. Render it
    right after the head and in the same block: the layout's content column puts a
    gap between its direct children, and head and crumb are one thing.

    Why: one title rule for the whole area (doc/verwaltung_seitennavigation_plan.md
    §7a, 1). The title names the section on every page of it, so a page below the
    section keeps the section's head and names itself here. A title of its own with
    the way back under it would be a second rule.

    No rule of its own. Under a tab row the bar's rule is right above it, and a
    second line one row lower would stack two. Under a bare title box the section
    page has no rule either, so the page below it reads as its section plus one line.

    The content keeps the distance it has below a head without a crumb: 24px below
    the title box (its mb-6), 14px below the tab row (the bar's own mb-3.5). The
    crumb repeats that gap under itself, and `[nav+&]` picks the bar's when the bar
    stands right above it. That follows what was rendered, not what was asked for:
    a section with a single tab gets no bar, and the crumb then follows the title box.
    Measured in spec/system/page_title_edge_spec.rb.

    A long file name breaks inside the line instead of widening the page. Below a
    file it is the way back, on the file's own page it is the page, and it may have
    no break point at all. The way back is a flex box, which never gets narrower
    than the longest unbroken piece of its text: overflow-wrap:anywhere lets the
    name break between any two characters where it would not fit otherwise, and so
    makes that piece a single character (break-words would not). The page item is
    plain text in a box that may shrink (min-w-0), so break-words is enough there.
    A short label looks as before. Measured on a phone in page_title_edge_spec.

    No horizontal margin or padding: the page edge belongs to the layout
    (spec/views/one_page_edge_guard_spec.rb). The page is the one
    aria-current="page"; a tab above it is marked as its parent (below_tab: true on
    the head). data-section-crumb is the hook for specs. %>
<nav aria-label="<%= t('navigation.section_crumb') %>" class="mb-6 [nav+&]:mb-3.5" data-section-crumb>
  <ol class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-2 text-sm">
    <li class="flex items-center gap-x-3">
      <%= link_to back_path, class: 'inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 font-medium text-cream-600 transition-colors [overflow-wrap:anywhere] hover:text-cream-900' do %>
        <span aria-hidden="true">‹</span><%= back_label %>
      <% end %>
      <span class="text-cream-400" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
    </li>
    <li class="min-w-0 break-words">
      <span class="font-bold text-cream-900" aria-current="page"><%= page %></span>
    </li>
  </ol>
</nav>
